@* A page's controls behind a button on its own bar, at every width.

   THE OTHER HALF OF PageRail
   ==================================================================================
   Two shells fold a page's controls away, and which one a page wants is decided by what the page
   is for. A Browse page is content you scan and narrow, so its controls earn a column beside that
   content and are worth a thumb's reach mid-scroll: <see cref="PageRail"/>. An Answer page leads
   with a verdict, and its controls set what the verdict is OF -- reached once on arrival and then
   not again -- so they fold behind a button on the bar and the page spends none of its width on
   them.

   What they SHARE is this file's reason to exist. The handle, the grabber, the Done button and
   the group headings were pasted into four pages as twelve lines of markup apiece, under a
   `target-sheet` class that had no stylesheet rule at all -- it did nothing but sit beside `sheet`,
   which is where the behaviour actually was. So the four drifted: not one of them grouped its
   controls under a heading, and two still carried a `bar-sep`, the vertical rule that separated
   items on the horizontal toolbar these replaced.

   Group the contents in `rail-group`, the same way a rail does, and for the same reason: a column
   has room for the heading, so the control says what it sets rather than leaving a reader to infer
   it from the options. Trades opened to five bare selects -- "Everything, Ranked", "Hardest to
   Pull", "Any Rarity" -- with nothing saying which was the grouping and which the sort.

   The opener is <see cref="SheetOpener"/> and goes in the bar's own actions, because a button
   inside this element would be a `.page-head .btn` and take the bar's button styling into the
   sheet. *@

<div class="page-sheet sheet" popover id="@Id">
    <SheetHandle For="@Id" Label="@Label" />

    @ChildContent
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// DOM id, which the opener points at. Unique per page: two popovers sharing one id means
    /// popovertarget resolves to the first and the second can never be opened.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Id { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// What this sheet is, for its own heading row. The same word the <see cref="SheetOpener"/>
    /// that opens it carries — they are two halves of one control, and a sheet titled differently
    /// from the button that produced it is a sheet you are not sure you meant to open.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string Label { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The groups themselves, in the order the sheet presents them.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
